    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to look closely at every detail when you miss out on a promotion—especially after five years as deputy news editor and fifteen years in the field. \n\nHowever, **the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women is not, on its own, evidence of bias or a predetermined outcome.** \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of how to evaluate this situation and what to do next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition\n* **Role-based appointments:** Selection panels at public broadcasters are usually determined by job titles (e.g., Head of News, HR Business Partner, Digital Director, Senior Output Editor) rather than an arbitrary selection of individuals. If women currently hold those specific leadership posts, the panel will naturally be all female.\n* **Internal policies:** Many public broadcasters have recruitment policies governing panel diversity (often requiring a mix of genders or backgrounds). An all-female panel may simply reflect staffing availability, but you can check whether your organization’s recruitment code specifies gender balance on interview panels.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experience vs. Strategic Priorities\n* **Years of service vs. strategic fit:** While 15 years of experience (including 5 as deputy) is strong traditional qualification, public broadcasters are currently undergoing intense digital transformations. \n* **The digital focus:** The successful candidate’s 8 years of experience paired with \"strong experience of digital media\" suggests the panel prioritized modern digital transformation and audience shift over traditional broadcast seniority. In competitive shortlists, interview performance and strategic vision often outweigh raw tenure.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\n* In large public organizations, a two-week delay for formal written feedback is common. HR approvals, panel member sign-offs, and administrative backlogs often cause delays.\n* While frustrating, two weeks of silence is generally a sign of bureaucratic process rather than deliberate evasion.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Should You Make Something of It?\n**No—at least not at this stage.** Raising the gender composition of the panel as an issue without concrete evidence carries significant professional risks:\n* **High burden of proof:** An allegation of sex discrimination cannot rely solely on the panel being female and the successful candidate being female. Without documented procedural irregularities, inconsistent scoring, or discriminatory remarks, the claim will likely be dismissed.\n* **Reputational impact:** Newsrooms and public media sectors are tight-knit. Raising an accusation of gender bias that lacks solid evidentiary backing can damage professional standing and future advancement within the organization.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up professionally on your feedback request:**\n   Send a polite, constructive email to HR or the panel chair:\n   > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for written feedback regarding the News Editor interview. As someone deeply invested in the department, I am keen to understand the panel's assessment—particularly around areas where I can strengthen my profile for future senior editorial opportunities.\"*\n\n2. **Check internal hiring governance:**\n   Review your broadcaster’s internal recruitment code, collective agreements, or union guidelines regarding:\n   * Panel composition requirements.\n   * Timelines for candidate feedback.\n   * Rights to review scoring rubrics or interview notes.\n\n3. **Examine the substance of the feedback:**\n   Once you receive the written explanation, review the scoring criteria objectively. Look at how your strategic vision, leadership presentation, and digital competencies were evaluated relative to the job description.\n\nIf the eventual feedback reveals clear procedural breaches or demonstrable inconsistencies against the published selection criteria, that is the appropriate basis for an internal inquiry or union consultation—not the gender composition of the panel.\n",
